{"name":"Guppy Violet","hex":"#ae5883","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"guppy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A species of tiny freshwater fish, Poecilia reticulata, popular in home aquariums, that usually has a plain body and black or dark blue tail for the females and a more colorful tail for the males. The guppies can vary in size, but males are 4 cm (1.6”) and females are 7 cm (2.8”).","Any tiny fish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/guppy"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Guppy","blocks":[{"text":"The guppy, also known as the peacock fish, millionfish or rainbow fish, is a member of the family Poeciliidae and, akin to most New World members of the family, is classified as a livebearer. Male guppies, which are smaller than females, have ornamental caudal and dorsal fins. Wild guppies generally feed on a variety of food sources, including benthic algae and the larvae of aquatic insects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Guppy_pho_0048.jpg/330px-Guppy_pho_0048.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guppy","wikidata":"Q178202"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)","wikidata":"Q428124"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/500px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/1280px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/500px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sexual dimorphism exhibited in the Trinidadian guppy (above male, below female)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Guppy_breeds.jpg/500px-Guppy_breeds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Guppy_breeds.jpg/500px-Guppy_breeds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Six examples of guppy breeds in the aquarium hobby","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuppy_breeds.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magna.png/500px-Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magna.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magna.png/1280px-Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magna.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magna.png/500px-Guppy_Food_-_Daphnia_Magn